  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nAll is quiet weather-wise across northern Illinois and \nnorthwest Indiana early this morning with temperatures in the \n60s and increasing high cloud coverage. Attention is instead on\nmonitoring upstream features which will play a role in our \nshower and storm chances later today. Overall confidence is on \nthe lower side in how things unfold today owing to weaker \nforcing mechanisms for ascent initially in the afternoon and \nless favorable diurnal timing of the cold frontal passage this \nevening. This has led to continued variability from model run to\nrun in the location and coverage of storms later this afternoon\nthrough the evening.\n\nThere are currently a few different thunderstorm complexes to \nour west and northwest, including southeast Minnesota, \nsoutheast South Dakota and eastern Nebraska tied to mid-level \nimpulses moving within the broader northwest flow aloft. The \nMinnesota storms have been struggling to hold together for \nseveral hours and are likely to continue decaying with \nsoutheast extent across southern Wisconsin. The complex of \nstorms in Nebraska remains more organized early this morning but\nis expected to eventually weaken with eastward extent into Iowa\nand northern Missouri. However, it is possible that a remnant \nMCV feature within the northern stratiform rain area persists as\nit crosses Iowa this morning into the area by this afternoon. \nThis paired with a mid-level vort may lead to renewed shower \ndevelopment this afternoon near and north of I-80. Modeled \nsoundings suggest that the layer of steeper lapse rates will be \nfairly narrow through mid-afternoon suggesting that instability \nwill be limited and keep the lightning threat lower initially.\n\nFarther to the northwest a cold front is currently diving \nsoutheast across the SD/ND border and northwest MN. As this \nmoves into central Wisconsin later this afternoon it may lead \nto increasing thunderstorm coverage as the mid-level flow \nstrengthens to 45-55 kt. This combined with increasing PWATs \ninto the 1.5-2.0\" range supports an increasing damaging wind \nthreat from precip loaded downbursts with any storms that\ndevelop. This activity may then drift southeast into northern \nand northeast Illinois this evening (~5-10 PM timeframe). Given \nthe higher PWATs, locally heavy rainfall will likely accompany \nany storms that develop and could result in a localized flash \nflood threat, particularly if multiple cells move over the same \nareas. The slow southward progress of the cold front may lead to\nshowers and thunderstorms lingering into the early overnight \nhours south of I-80 before they fully exit the area toward \ndaybreak Wednesday.\n\nAnother break in this active pattern is in store midweek as a \ncooler and less humid airmass settles into the area. \nTemperatures will be slightly cooler for this time of year \n(typically lower 80s in mid August) with highs in the mid to \nupper 70s each day amidst cooler onshore flow. Confidence in the\ndetails then decreases late week into the upcoming weekend as \nadditional waves move within the upper level northwest flow \npattern which will bring periodic shower and storm chances into \nthe weekend.\n\nPetr",
